---
// Privacy page. Section 3 also says the site counts clicks on its email address (event
// "Email Tap", src/components/EmailLink.astro; a goal of that name is set in the
// instance). The visible text cites no articles or statutes, in plain words like
// m-squad.com/privacy (owner, 2026-10-12); tests/a11y.spec.ts holds that. Decided:
//   1. The controller is whoever legally runs the site: M-Squad GmbH (filled in on
//      9 October 2026; name, address and managing director as in the imprint).
//   2. The live build runs Plausible on M-Squad's own instance (ANALYTICS in
//      src/config.ts, configured 12 October 2026). Section 3 and the meta description
//      below say so. If the instance or the tool changes, rewrite both first.
//      Section 3's sentences on clicks and downloads mirror two settings in the
//      instance (Settings > General > Tracking: Outbound links and File downloads
//      ticked, Form submissions unticked because the site has no form). When those
//      ticks change, change the sentences; if a form is ever added, decide on the
//      third. What the instance keeps, and for how long, is the owner's to confirm:
//      README, "Launch requirements".
//      The page states no consent conclusion on purpose. Running without a banner is
//      a position, not a settled fact. The owner accepted it on 2026-10-12 (the README
//      records who and how); no counsel's view was taken. The script sets no cookies and writes
//      nothing to the device. It sends the page address and the referrer, scroll
//      depth, engaged time, and the address of clicked outbound links and downloaded
//      files. It also reads one local-storage flag (plausible_ignore, which excludes
//      your own visits) and navigator.webdriver (a bot check); neither is sent. The
//      EDPB (Guidelines 2/2023 v2.0, para 33) says JavaScript that instructs the
//      browser to send information "clearly falls within the scope of Article
//      5(3)"; para 53 treats information that client-side code sends back to a
//      server as a gaining of access; para 56 adds that this does not by itself
//      make consent necessary. § 25 TDDDG has two exceptions in Abs. 2 and none for
//      statistics. The DSK (OH Digitale Dienste v1.2, Nov 2024) makes no statement
//      that reach measurement is consent-free (Rz. 87 to 89), at most for a
//      precisely defined configuration that stops holding when more information is
//      added, and says even simple counting is not automatically part of the basic
//      service (Rz. 90). Plausible's own legal assessment concludes from "does not
//      store any information on the user's device" and does not discuss the script
//      reading the page address or the referrer. This configuration measures more
//      than bare counting. Any added embed, tool or measured data needs this page
//      and that position re-checked before it ships.
// Still open:
//   3. German-market sites: translate this page into German (Datenschutzerklaerung)
//      and serve it at /datenschutz; German law expects legal pages in German.
//   4. A solid baseline, not legal advice. When in doubt, have it reviewed.
// The copy below already passes the a11y / seo / tone / email suites: keep one
// <h1>, the description in the 120-160 char window, no em dashes or banned
// contractions, and every email address behind <EmailLink>.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailLink from '../components/EmailLink.astro';
import { SITE, COMPANY } from '../config';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles personal data: cookieless analytics, hosting on Cloudflare Pages, contact data handling, and your rights.`}>
  <section class="wrap">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>
      Last updated: 13 October 2026. This page explains which personal data this
      website processes, why, and which rights you have.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Controller</h2>
    <p>The following company is responsible for this website and for the data processing described on this page:</p>
    <p>
      {COMPANY.legalName}<br />
      {COMPANY.street}<br />
      {COMPANY.postcodeCity}<br />
      Managing director: {COMPANY.managingDirector}<br />
      Contact: <EmailLink address={COMPANY.email} subject="Privacy request" />
    </p>
    <p>More company details are in our <a href="/imprint">imprint</a>.</p>

    <h2>2. Hosting and server logs</h2>
    <p>
      This site is served by <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> (Cloudflare, Inc.,
      101 Townsend St, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA). When you open a page,
      Cloudflare processes technical connection data: your IP address, the date
      and time, the requested address, and your browser type and version.
      Cloudflare needs
      this data to deliver the site and to protect it against attacks.
    </p>
    <p>
      We process this data because we have a legitimate interest in a secure and
      fast website. Cloudflare works on our behalf. Transfers to the USA rely on
      the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses. We do
      not combine these logs with any other data.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Cookies and analytics</h2>
    <p>
      This website is ad-free and <strong>sets no cookies</strong>. Your visit
      leaves no advertising or cross-site tracking identifiers with us.
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand how the site is used, we run <strong>Plausible
      Analytics</strong>, a web analytics tool, on our own server at
      analytics.m-squad.com, operated by {COMPANY.legalName}. Its script sets no
      cookies and writes nothing to your device. It checks one setting in your
      browser, which lets you exclude your own visits, and whether the browser
      is automated; neither is sent to our analytics server.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you open a page, the script sends the address of that page and the
      page or site you came from to our analytics server, together with the technical
      data your browser sends with every request (browser, operating system,
      device type, IP address). When you leave the page or switch to another tab or window, it also sends how far
      you scrolled and how long the page was visible and in focus. It also
      counts clicks on links to other websites and sends the address of the link
      you clicked. It counts clicks on links to files, such as the slide deck,
      and sends the address of the file. We also count how often an email
      address on this site is clicked, together with the page it was on; the
      address itself is not sent.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool derives your approximate location from the IP address and does not keep the IP address in its own records. From your IP address, your browser data
      and this website's domain, plus a value that is replaced every 24 hours,
      it forms an identifier. While that value lasts, and as long as those inputs stay the same, the tool therefore recognises the same browser and counts it as one visitor. Once the value is replaced, it can no longer recognise that browser; only a visit that is still going at that moment (one with activity in the last 30 minutes) continues as the same visit. Nor can the tool recognise that browser on another website. The individual events (for example each page view, and the scroll depth and engaged time sent when you leave a page or switch away from it) and the session records are stored with this identifier, the page address, the referrer, any link or file address sent with the event, the location and the device data, and are shown as aggregated counts, for example page views, referrers and countries. Requests to our analytics server pass
      through Cloudflare, which also serves this site (see section 2).
    </p>
    <p>
      We run this measurement because we have a legitimate interest in
      understanding how the website is used and in improving it. The individual
      events and session records are deleted from the measurement server after 24
      months. Encrypted backup copies of this data are kept for 30 days, so a deleted record can remain in a backup for up to 30 days longer. A backup copy also holds the value that was current when it was made. You can prevent the measurement by blocking the analytics script
      in your browser, for example with a content blocker. Our analytics do not
      respond to your browser's &ldquo;Do Not Track&rdquo; setting.
    </p>
    <p>
      This website loads no fonts from outside: it uses the fonts already on your
      device. Its images come from this website itself, not from a third-party
      service. Apart from this website's own address, your browser contacts only
      our analytics server.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Contact by email or phone</h2>
    <p>
      When you contact us by email or phone, we process the data you share: your
      name, your email address or phone number, and the content of your message
      or call. We use it to handle your request and any follow-up questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your request is about a contract, or about preparing one, we process
      your data because we need it for that. Otherwise we rely on our legitimate
      interest in answering enquiries. We keep correspondence, and any note we
      make of a call, only as long as we need it for this purpose and for any
      legal retention duties.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Your rights</h2>
    <p>You decide what happens with your data. You can ask us at any time for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>information about which data we hold about you,</li>
      <li>correction of wrong details,</li>
      <li>deletion of your data, for example your name, email address or phone number from an enquiry,</li>
      <li>restriction of processing,</li>
      <li>your data in a common, machine-readable format.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Write to us by <EmailLink address={COMPANY.email} subject="Privacy request" text="email" /> to do
      so. If we doubt that a request comes from you, we ask you to confirm who
      you are first. We cannot look up or delete technical connection data that
      we cannot link to a person. You can also complain to a data protection
      supervisory authority. The authority responsible for us is the Bavarian
      State Office for Data Protection Supervision (BayLDA), Promenade 18, 91522
      Ansbach, Germany.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Right to object:</strong> where we base the processing of your
      data on our legitimate interest, you can object for reasons arising from
      your particular situation. Write to us by
      <EmailLink address={COMPANY.email} subject="Privacy request" text="email" /> to do so.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Changes to this page</h2>
    <p>
      We update this page whenever the data processing on this website changes,
      and we adjust the date at the top.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
